   "text": {
    "L1": "Every triangle has three angles, and their total is always 180 degrees.",
    "L2": "The triangle may be large or small, but its angle sum does not change.",
    "L3": "A parallel line through one vertex makes alternate angles equal to the other two angles.",
    "L4": "Together, the three angles lie on a straight line, which is 180 degrees.",
    "L5": "In the worked example, angle P is 48 degrees and angle Q is twice P, so Q is 96 degrees.",
    "L6": "The first two angles total 144 degrees, so angle R is 180 minus 144: 36 degrees.",
    "L7": "Here is a myth: a triangle can have two obtuse angles. Two 100-degree angles already total 200 degrees, so no third angle can fit.",
    "L8": "Try this: two angles are 50 degrees and 60 degrees. Find the third angle."
   },
